<p class="now-as-of">As of 11 November 2026.</p>
<section class="story-section" id="problem"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The problem before</p><h2>A bigger brain still<br />needs the right help.</h2><p>A delivery planner can try a hundred routes and still fail if it never receives the address. It can read yesterday’s map perfectly and still hit a gate that closed this morning.</p><p>We have met models that read more, use tools and spend longer answering. How do those pieces fit together?</p></section>
<section class="story-section" id="idea"><p class="eyebrow">02 / The big idea</p><h2>Read. Check. Try again.</h2><p><strong>Context</strong> is the information supplied for the current answer. A <strong>tool</strong> is a program the system can call for information or an action. <strong>Inference compute</strong> is the computation spent producing an answer. These resources do different jobs.</p><p>In 2026, that combination is visible across model families. DeepSeek-V4 and V4.1-Flash support million-token contexts; a <strong>token</strong> is a piece of text. Gemini 4 Argon’s model card describes text, image, audio and video input sharing a million-token window. Anthropic’s Opus 5.5 and OpenAI’s GPT-6 Astra announcements emphasize tool-using work. These are examples of the combination, not a ranking. Newer releases continue the pattern: Google describes Gemini 4 Carbon (28 October) as built for sustained agentic workflows, and Anthropic’s Claude Fable 5.2 (9 November) can coordinate smaller models as an orchestrator.</p><p>Keeping more context also costs memory. Our anchor paper, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, tackles that cost by reusing and compressing stored attention information. The broader lesson: making a useful system means improving how its parts work together.</p>
<figure class="idea-diagram now-diagram"><svg viewBox="0 0 480 310" role="img" aria-labelledby="now-diagram-title now-diagram-desc"><title id="now-diagram-title">Three resources meet in one answer</title><desc id="now-diagram-desc">Context supplies the address, a tool supplies the gate status, and answer-time effort tries routes. A failed check can lead to another attempt. This is a conceptual illustration.</desc><g class="diagram-outline"><rect x="15" y="20" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/><rect x="255" y="20" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/><rect x="135" y="140" width="210" height="64" rx="8"/></g><g fill="currentColor" text-anchor="middle"><text x="120" y="59">Context: address</text><text x="360" y="59">Tool: gate status</text><text x="240" y="179">Effort: try routes</text></g><path d="M120 90V110H360V90M240 110V132M240 210V250M232 242L240 250L248 242M350 280H440V172H353M361 164L353 172L361 180" class="diagram-line"/><rect x="135" y="255" width="210" height="48" rx="8" class="diagram-fill"/><text x="240" y="286" text-anchor="middle" fill="currentColor">A checked answer</text></svg><figcaption>Information and effort are complementary. Extra attempts cannot supply a missing fact.</figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="story-section toy-section" id="play"><p class="eyebrow">03 / Play with it</p><h2>Get the parcel there.<br />Spend as little as you can.</h2><ChapterBrief slug="now"/><p>Every note, tool call and route attempt costs credits, and each town closes different roads. You can see the closures; the planner can’t, unless you pay for the road-status tool. Deliver for the cheapest possible price to earn three stars.</p><noscript><p>Enable JavaScript to assemble the planner. Send the address, decide whether the planner needs fresh road status, and give it enough route attempts to find a delivery.</p></noscript><NowToy client:load /></section>
<section class="story-section" id="mattered"><p class="eyebrow">04 / Why it mattered</p><h2>The system is<br />part of the story.</h2><p>Look beyond the model’s name: what evidence reaches it, what can it check, and what work happens before the answer? The toy lets you separate those questions. Real systems must also learn when to use each resource, and can still make mistakes.</p><p>That is where this timeline stops for now. It began in 1913 with a much smaller question: which letter comes next?</p></section>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="deeper"><summary>Go deeper <span>Context memory, effort and limits</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><h3>What is being compressed?</h3><p>A KV cache stores keys and values: attention representations retained for reuse while generating. It is temporary computation state, not the model’s learned weights. V4.1-Flash combines cross-layer reuse in Compressed Sparse Attention 2 with FP4 storage, a four-bit numerical format. Its causal encoder-decoder design uses different amounts of active computation when reading input and generating output.</p><h3>One result, carefully interpreted</h3><p>The report gives 890 bytes per token for its global KV cache, about one quarter of V4-Flash’s corresponding footprint. That is a specific cache comparison. It is not total device memory, an overall speedup, or proof of equal quality on every task. Persistent storage is discussed separately in the paper.</p><h3>Two other ways to make reading efficient</h3><p><strong>FlashAttention (2022)</strong> computes exact attention in blocks, moving less data between a graphics processor’s fast local memory and its larger main memory. It avoids storing the full attention-score matrix there; this is different from compressing a generation-time KV cache. <strong>Mamba (2023)</strong> uses a selective state space model: a running state whose update depends on the current input. Its architecture replaces attention and uses a parallel algorithm during training. It offers another approach to long sequences, with different tradeoffs. These papers are alternatives to explore, not claims about the internals of every model named above.</p><h3>Different resources, different costs</h3><p>For ordinary dense multi-head attention, an illustrative KV storage estimate is <code>M = 2 × L × T × Hkv × d × b / 8</code> bytes: L layers, T stored tokens, Hkv key/value heads, d values per head and b bits per value. The factor two counts keys and values. Sharing heads, compressing states or reusing them across layers changes this estimate; it does not describe V4.1-Flash’s architecture.</p><p>A longer context limit does not guarantee that a model uses every detail correctly. More answer-time computation cannot guarantee correctness either. Tool results can be stale or wrong, and an agent can choose the wrong tool. Gemini’s model card notes practical limits; ReAct and the R1 report describe particular methods and evaluations, not universal guarantees.</p><h3>What the delivery planner does</h3><p>The planner receives the last n notes. It requires the address, then checks up to k fixed routes, stopping at the first that matches the known road status, destination and seven-minute deadline. Without a lookup it assumes the old map’s open roads. A separate outcome check uses the town’s actual closures, which are picked at random each game, exposing a plan that looked valid but fails. Each note and route attempt costs one credit and a lookup costs two; these invented prices stand in for the idea that context, tools and effort are not free. They are not tokens, FLOPs, latency or real prices. No training, attention compression or language generation runs here.</p></div></details>
<details class="chapter-disclosure" id="sources"><summary>Sources <span>Primary papers and official model reports</span></summary><div class="disclosure-body"><ol class="source-list"><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-v4-1')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash · September 2026</a><p>Abstract and Sections 1–3: million-token context, encoder-decoder structure, cross-layer cache reuse, FP4 storage and the global-cache comparison. Section 5 discusses reasoning effort and agent evaluations. This is the chapter’s anchor paper.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-v4')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-V4 · 2026</a><p>Abstract and architecture sections: million-token context and compressed attention.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('gemini-4-argon')}>Google DeepMind · Gemini 4 Argon model card · October 2026</a><p>Model Information: input modalities and a combined input-output context of up to one million tokens. Intended Usage and Limitations: remaining model limitations and links to family documentation.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('claude-opus-5-5')}>Anthropic · Introducing Claude Opus 5.5 · September 2026</a><p>Introduction and Performance: tool-using work, evaluation conditions and limits of benchmark comparisons. No cross-vendor ranking is inferred here.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('gpt-6-astra')}>OpenAI · GPT-6 Astra · September 2026</a><p>Introduction and computer-use sections: tool-using workflows. The examples here do not infer equivalence with other models or adopt vendor rankings.</p></li><li><a href="https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/models-and-research/gemini-models/introducing-gemini-4-carbon/">Google · Gemini 4 Carbon: our next frontier model · October 2026</a><p>Launch post: sustained agentic workflows, long-horizon engineering tasks and a staged rollout starting with cyber defenders.</p></li><li><a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/claude-fable-and-mythos-5-2">Anthropic · Introducing Claude Fable 5.2 and Claude Mythos 5.2 · November 2026</a><p>Announcement: one model offered in two configurations, with Fable 5.2 able to act as an orchestrator that delegates simpler tasks to smaller models.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('flash-attention')}>Dao et al. · FlashAttention · 2022</a><p>Abstract, Figure 1 and Section 3: exact attention using tiling and recomputation to reduce memory traffic and avoid materializing the attention matrix in main device memory.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('mamba')}>Gu and Dao · Mamba · 2023</a><p>Abstract and Section 3: input-dependent state updates, an attention-free architecture and a hardware-aware parallel recurrent algorithm.</p></li><li><a href={sourceLink('vaswani-2017-neurips-full-text')}>Vaswani et al. · Attention Is All You Need · 2017</a><p>Section 3: keys, values, multi-head attention and recurrence-free sequence processing. The storage expression above counts uncompressed keys and values; it is an explanatory calculation.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('yao-2022-react-synergizing-reasoning-acting-language-models')}>Yao et al. · ReAct · 2022</a><p>Method and experiments: actions return observations for subsequent decisions, with remaining failure modes.</p></li><li><a href={paperLink('deepseek-ai-2025-deepseek-r1-incentivizes-reasoning-reinforcement-learning')}>DeepSeek-AI · DeepSeek-R1 · 2025</a><p>Training and evaluation sections: learning reasoning behavior and evaluating answer-time effort. This motivates the distinction between learning and trying more candidates.</p></li></ol><p>Also in this chapter: DeepSeek-V4, Gemini 4 Argon, Claude Opus 5.5 and GPT-6 Astra; FlashAttention and Mamba offer related efficiency ideas. The synthesis, parcel, notes, map, candidate routes and diagram are teaching illustrations. They are not outputs or benchmarks from these models.</p></div></details>
